config MESHTASTIC_PKI
	bool "PKC for direct messages (Curve25519/X25519 + AES-CCM)"
	depends on MESHTASTIC_NODEDB && MESHTASTIC_SETTINGS
	help
	  Implements Meshtastic public-key cryptography for direct messages,
	  interoperable with the reference firmware: an X25519 keypair (generated
	  once, persisted in the SecurityConfig NVS partition — survives reboot and
	  reflash), advertised via User.public_key, with AES-256-CCM DM
	  encrypt/decrypt (key = SHA256(ECDH shared secret)). Fixes both the
	  "PKI send failed, no public key" a peer sees and the NO_CHANNEL a peer
	  returns for our PSK-only DMs.

	  Requires PSA crypto: ECDH + ECC Montgomery-255 (key pair
	  generate/import/export + public key), AES, CCM and SHA-256. On the net
	  build the ECC/AES/SHA pieces come from the TLS overlay; the overlay adds
	  PSA_WANT_ALG_CCM.

config MESHTASTIC_NODE_ID_FROM_KEY
	bool "Derive the node ID from the identity public key (reference >= 2.8)"
	depends on MESHTASTIC_PKI
	select CRC
	help
	  Use crc32(X25519 public key) as the node ID, as the reference firmware
	  has since 2.8 (NodeDB::createNewIdentity, shipped with packet signing).
	  The id then commits to the key: nobody can claim an id without also
	  holding the key it was derived from, which is what makes trusting a
	  stranger's first signed NodeInfo safe.

	  MESHTASTIC_NODE_ID_SOURCE still supplies the PROVISIONAL id, used only
	  when no key is readable and none was stored. The adopted id is kept in
	  the unsealed "mtid" settings record so a lockdown-locked boot, which
	  cannot read the keypair, keeps the same id.

	  Turning this on CHANGES the id of an existing node once, on its first
	  boot, and every peer then holds the node under both ids until the old
	  entry is forgotten or ages out (the reference does the same). Keys do
	  not change, so the new id is predictable before flashing, and turning
	  it off again returns the node to its hardware id.
